WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.250
Zainab added up her class's

2
00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:03.510
marks with a calculator.

3
00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.190
Forty numbers, three times, and

4
00:00:07.190 --> 00:00:08.630
three different answers!

5
00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:10.490
There's a better way.

6
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.660
Today: spreadsheets, formulas, and

7
00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:16.240
your first lines of Python.

8
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:19.800
A spreadsheet is a big table.

9
00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.350
Columns have letters: A, B, C.

10
00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:28.740
Rows have numbers: one, two, three.

11
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.230
So every cell has an address,

12
00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.810
like B2: column B, row two.

13
00:00:36.030 --> 00:00:38.710
A formula starts with an equals sign.

14
00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:42.630
SUM of B2 to B9 adds up

15
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:44.330
eight cells: a range.

16
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.900
AVERAGE finds the mean, and COUNTIF

17
00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:49.580
counts the cells that match.

18
00:00:50.910 --> 00:00:52.690
And when a mark changes, the

19
00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:54.170
formula updates by itself.

20
00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:57.880
No adding up again, and no new mistakes.

21
00:00:58.790 --> 00:01:01.150
But it only works if the table is

22
00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:03.710
tidy: one value in each cell.

23
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:05.460
Quick check!

24
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:10.710
What does SUM of B2 to B5 add up?

25
00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:17.420
B2, B3, B4 and B5: the colon means

26
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.060
everything from B2 to B5.

27
00:01:20.790 --> 00:01:21.930
That's called a range.

28
00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:24.730
Programs can do this too.

29
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.520
In Python, a list holds the

30
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:29.180
marks in square brackets.

31
00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:32.940
sum adds them up, and len counts them.

32
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:35.680
Divide one by the other, and

33
00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:36.540
you have the average.

34
00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.650
Formulas and programs both need tidy data.

35
00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:45.100
A mark typed as fourteen slash seventeen

36
00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.880
is text, and text can't be added.

37
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.680
A Total row inside the data

38
00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.220
gets counted as a pupil.

39
00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:55.830
In the app, Mr Bello's marks sheet

40
00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.630
gets three questions wrong.

41
00:01:58.260 --> 00:02:01.580
Fix the table one tap at a time, and watch

42
00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:03.400
the computer's answers turn right.

43
00:02:04.660 --> 00:02:05.420
Your turn.

44
00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.180
Why is a formula better than

45
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.640
typing the total yourself?

46
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.240
It updates by itself when a mark changes.

47
00:02:14.050 --> 00:02:15.970
Remember: every cell has

48
00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.790
an address, like B2.

49
00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:20.370
Formulas start with equals,

50
00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:21.970
and work on ranges.

51
00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.000
And in Python, sum and

52
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:26.660
len do the same job.

53
00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:30.260
Try it tomorrow: make a table of

54
00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:31.660
something you track for a week,

55
00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:33.280
like hours of sleep.

56
00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:36.770
Add a total and an average with formulas.

57
00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:39.750
Then change one number, and

58
00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:40.730
watch them update.
